@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for Linux (64-bit)
======================================================

Raspberry Pi: this needs the 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS. On the 32-bit one,
"Play @TITLE@" can't start (the system says it can't execute
it, or that the file isn't found). In a terminal, "getconf LONG_BIT" says
64 on a 64-bit system.

@TITLE@ (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run natively: the game's own
program translated into C++ and compiled for your computer, drawn with
Vulkan.

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the game: @RELEASE@,
its disc image extracted to a folder (for example with extract-xiso or
xdvdfs). That gives a folder holding default.xbe and the folders bundles,
demand, xwavebank and the rest.

This is a work in progress: the game plays, and some things are still
missing or wrong. Changes: CHANGELOG.md on the project's page.


Playing
-------
1. Unpack the download anywhere you can write to (your home folder is
   fine): double-click it in your file manager, or in a terminal
   "tar xzf @PACKAGE@-*.tar.gz". Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open the @PACKAGE@ folder and double-click "Play @TITLE@"
   (if your file manager asks, choose Execute or Run). From a terminal:
   ./"Play @TITLE@"
3. Click "Choose your disc folder" and pick the folder you extracted the
   disc into (the one holding default.xbe), or drag the folder onto the
   window. (Where the desktop has no folder chooser, type or paste the
   folder's path.) It tells you straight away if it's the wrong game or
   another release.
4. Wait for the first build: a few minutes on a desktop PC. Your game
   files never leave your computer.
5. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs. "Add to the
   applications menu" puts @TITLE@ in your desktop's menu.

After that, Play @TITLE@ remembers your disc folder (even in a new
download folder), and Play starts the game straight away. After an update
it builds the game again first, recompiling only what changed.

The game's output is in work/run.log and work/run-errors.log, and the
build's in work/build.log. Send them when reporting a problem. If the
picture is wrong, press F9 while it shows: the next 12 frames and what the
game drew in them are saved to a file in the data folder's captures folder
(the game says where). Send that file too.

Needs: a 64-bit Linux on x86-64 (the linux-x86_64 download) or ARM64 (the
linux-arm64 download) with glibc 2.35 or later (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12,
Fedora 36, Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm or later), and a graphics driver with
Vulkan 1.3: Mesa's Vulkan drivers (on Debian and Ubuntu, the
mesa-vulkan-drivers package) or NVIDIA's. Before building, Play
@TITLE@ checks that there is one and says what to install if not.
Updates use curl (or wget) and tar, which nearly every Linux has.


Raspberry Pi
------------
- The 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS (see the top of this file), on a Raspberry Pi
  4 or 5 with 2 GB of memory or more. Use the linux-arm64 download.
- Vulkan 1.3 for the Pi's graphics arrived with Mesa 24.3: Raspberry Pi OS
  Trixie (October 2025) or later has it. On an older Raspberry Pi OS, Play
  @TITLE@ says so before building: update Raspberry Pi OS.
- The first build compiles the whole game on the Pi itself, so it takes a
  while: much longer than on a desktop PC, and longer on a Pi 4 than a Pi
  5, and on a 2 GB Pi, which compiles fewer files at a time. Leave it
  running. Later builds take compiled files from the cache.
- Copy the extracted disc onto the Pi (its SD card or a USB drive) first:
  the game reads its files from that folder while it runs.
- "Add to the applications menu" (once the game is built) puts @TITLE@
  in the Raspberry Pi menu, under Games.


Controls
--------
Any controller SDL knows (Xbox, PlayStation, Switch, generic), as the Xbox
controller, or the keyboard as controller 1:

  Xbox controller      Keyboard
  left stick           W A S D
  right stick          numeric keypad 8 4 5 6
  D-pad                arrows
  A B X Y              Space or K, L, J, I
  White, Black         Q, E   (left and right shoulder buttons)
  triggers             Left Shift, Right Shift or O
  Start, Back          Enter, Backspace or Tab
  stick clicks         F, H

F1 or Esc, or Back and Start together on a controller: the settings menu
(the game pauses): the display, the controls and the game's sound and
language. F11 or Alt+Enter: full screen.

Saves and settings are kept in ~/.local/share/qilin/@GAME@ (or
$XDG_DATA_HOME/qilin/@GAME@; the settings in ghoulies.ini), with the disc
folder you chose and the build's cache of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in here
--------------
Play @TITLE@  chooses your disc folder, builds the game (once) and
                         starts it
kit/qilin           the recompiler: turns the disc's @BOOT@ into C++
kit/lib, include    the prebuilt rest of the game: its runtime, the Xbox's
                    kernel, Direct3D and DirectSound replaced natively, the
                    renderer (Vulkan), the window, controls and sound (SDL3),
                    the settings menu (Dear ImGui)
kit/toolchain/zig   zig, a C++ compiler (Clang, with its own libc++)
kit/icon.png        the menu entry's icon
kit/files.txt       every file this release holds (see below)
work/               made when building: the game (@EXE_LINUX@), built from
                    your disc, the recompiled code, the build's files and
                    the logs
LICENSES/           the licences of everything included

zig keeps its own cache of compiled libraries in ~/.cache/zig.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/@REPO@


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in Play @TITLE@ asks GitHub for a newer release
(it sends nothing about you or your computer). If there is one, it
downloads it, unpacks it beside this folder and swaps in the new kit and
launcher, then starts again and builds the game for the new version. If
anything fails on the way, the old version is left as it was. You can also
unpack a new download anywhere, even over this folder: it finds your disc
folder and the cache by itself, and at its first start it removes files the
new kit no longer has (anything in kit/ or LICENSES/ that kit/files.txt
doesn't name). Your saves and settings are never in this folder, so nothing
of yours is touched.

The game is compiled for this computer's processor; moved to another
computer, it is built again. Compiled files come from the cache when
nothing they're made from has changed. A failed build leaves the working
game as it was; the one before a new build is kept as
work/@EXE_LINUX@.previous until the new one has run and closed normally.
"Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.

Before changing an existing title-save file, the runtime keeps five copies
under the data folder: save-history/partition1/<relative file>/1.bin
through 5.bin (1 is the newest). If a copy can't be made, the write is
refused. To restore one: quit the game, keep a separate copy of the current
partition1, and copy the chosen snapshot back to partition1/<relative
file>.
